1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,101
The most expensive part of a Formula

2
00:00:03,101 --> 00:00:05,686
One car is one you will almost

3
00:00:05,686 --> 00:00:06,548
never see.

4
00:00:06,728 --> 00:00:08,360
It is not the engine and it

5
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:09,932
is not the steering wheel.

6
00:00:10,112 --> 00:00:11,374
It is the floor,

7
00:00:11,374 --> 00:00:14,056
a flat sheet of carbon fibre under

8
00:00:14,056 --> 00:00:17,053
the car, so important that teams spend

9
00:00:17,053 --> 00:00:20,366
millions of dollars designing it and guard

10
00:00:20,366 --> 00:00:22,732
its shape like a state secret.

11
00:00:22,912 --> 00:00:25,693
Today, ten parts that make an F1

12
00:00:25,693 --> 00:00:27,692
car stick to the track,

13
00:00:27,692 --> 00:00:29,777
and why each one exists,

14
00:00:29,777 --> 00:00:31,863
starting with the floor.

15
00:00:32,214 --> 00:00:35,039
A Formula One car is a fighter

16
00:00:35,039 --> 00:00:37,206
jet turned upside down.

17
00:00:37,386 --> 00:00:39,715
A wing on an airplane pushes it

18
00:00:39,715 --> 00:00:39,940
up.

19
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:42,144
Every wing on an F1 car is

20
00:00:42,144 --> 00:00:44,091
designed to push it down,

21
00:00:44,091 --> 00:00:45,259
into the track,

22
00:00:45,259 --> 00:00:47,673
so the tyres can grip harder in

23
00:00:47,673 --> 00:00:48,607
the corners.

24
00:00:48,787 --> 00:00:51,225
That push is called downforce,

25
00:00:51,225 --> 00:00:53,582
and at high speed a modern F1

26
00:00:53,582 --> 00:00:56,589
car makes more downforce than its own

27
00:00:56,589 --> 00:00:57,158
weight.

28
00:00:57,338 --> 00:01:00,237
In theory, it could drive upside down

29
00:01:00,237 --> 00:01:02,353
on the ceiling of a tunnel.

30
00:01:02,704 --> 00:01:05,815
As always, every fact here comes from

31
00:01:05,815 --> 00:01:07,076
a named source,

32
00:01:07,076 --> 00:01:08,842
the rules of the FIA,

33
00:01:08,842 --> 00:01:12,627
Formula One itself and motorsport references,

34
00:01:12,627 --> 00:01:15,149
all listed in the description.

35
00:01:15,329 --> 00:01:17,814
Let's lift the car and look underneath.

36
00:01:18,164 --> 00:01:20,406
Why is the floor of an F1

37
00:01:20,406 --> 00:01:22,111
car worth millions?

38
00:01:22,291 --> 00:01:25,263
Because the floor is a giant wing.

39
00:01:25,443 --> 00:01:26,434
Under the car,

40
00:01:26,434 --> 00:01:28,910
two long tunnels run from the front

41
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:30,679
of the floor to the back.

42
00:01:30,859 --> 00:01:32,857
As air rushes through them,

43
00:01:32,857 --> 00:01:35,446
the tunnels narrow and then open up

44
00:01:35,446 --> 00:01:37,813
again, and the air speeds up and

45
00:01:37,813 --> 00:01:39,218
its pressure drops.

46
00:01:39,398 --> 00:01:41,599
Low pressure under the car,

47
00:01:41,599 --> 00:01:43,473
normal pressure on top.

48
00:01:43,653 --> 00:01:46,191
The whole car gets sucked down onto

49
00:01:46,191 --> 00:01:46,916
the track.

50
00:01:47,096 --> 00:01:49,023
That is called ground effect.

51
00:01:49,374 --> 00:01:52,320
Ground effect came back to Formula One

52
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:55,422
with the new rules of twenty twenty-two.

53
00:01:55,602 --> 00:01:58,820
Engineers estimate that the floor makes a

54
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:02,038
large share of the car's total downforce,

55
00:02:02,038 --> 00:02:05,020
by many estimates more than any single

56
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:05,413
wing.

57
00:02:05,593 --> 00:02:07,288
And it does it more cleanly.

58
00:02:07,468 --> 00:02:10,469
Wings on top leave turbulent air behind

59
00:02:10,469 --> 00:02:12,932
the car, which makes it hard for

60
00:02:12,932 --> 00:02:14,933
the next driver to follow.

61
00:02:15,113 --> 00:02:18,196
Downforce from the floor leaves less of

62
00:02:18,196 --> 00:02:19,381
that dirty air,

63
00:02:19,381 --> 00:02:21,278
so cars can race closer.

64
00:02:21,628 --> 00:02:24,255
That is why teams cover the floor

65
00:02:24,255 --> 00:02:26,963
with sheets when the car is lifted

66
00:02:26,963 --> 00:02:30,387
in public, and why photographers are chased

67
00:02:30,387 --> 00:02:33,413
away from cars hanging on cranes after

68
00:02:33,413 --> 00:02:34,050
a crash.

69
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:36,724
A few millimetres of change in the

70
00:02:36,724 --> 00:02:38,924
floor can be worth tenths of a

71
00:02:38,924 --> 00:02:40,024
second per lap.

72
00:02:40,204 --> 00:02:43,563
Teams build new versions through the season,

73
00:02:43,563 --> 00:02:46,694
each one designed in computers and tested

74
00:02:46,694 --> 00:02:48,145
in the wind tunnel.

75
00:02:48,325 --> 00:02:50,937
The sheet of carbon fibre is cheap.

76
00:02:51,117 --> 00:02:54,078
The knowledge inside its shape is not.

77
00:02:54,428 --> 00:02:56,884
Why is there a wooden plank under

78
00:02:56,884 --> 00:02:58,224
a Formula One car?

79
00:02:58,404 --> 00:03:01,218
Under the floor of every F1 car

80
00:03:01,218 --> 00:03:04,395
runs a long strip called the plank,

81
00:03:04,395 --> 00:03:05,666
or skid block,

82
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:08,389
made of a wood based material.

83
00:03:08,569 --> 00:03:11,706
It was introduced in nineteen ninety-four,

84
00:03:11,706 --> 00:03:14,096
the year of the deaths of Ayrton

85
00:03:14,096 --> 00:03:17,008
Senna and Roland Ratzenberger at Imola,

86
00:03:17,008 --> 00:03:19,249
as one of the measures to slow

87
00:03:19,249 --> 00:03:20,295
the cars down.

88
00:03:20,475 --> 00:03:22,593
The plank forces the car to run

89
00:03:22,593 --> 00:03:24,097
higher off the ground.

90
00:03:24,447 --> 00:03:26,814
The trick is that the plank works

91
00:03:26,814 --> 00:03:28,464
like a measuring stick.

92
00:03:28,644 --> 00:03:31,102
It starts ten millimetres thick,

93
00:03:31,102 --> 00:03:33,560
and after the race it must still

94
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:36,402
be at least nine millimetres thick at

95
00:03:36,402 --> 00:03:37,247
set points.

96
00:03:37,427 --> 00:03:39,439
If a team runs the car too

97
00:03:39,439 --> 00:03:41,838
low to gain more ground effect,

98
00:03:41,838 --> 00:03:44,470
the plank scrapes on the track and

99
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:45,321
wears away.

100
00:03:45,501 --> 00:03:46,670
Too much wear,

101
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:49,008
and the car is disqualified.

102
00:03:49,188 --> 00:03:50,977
In nineteen ninety-four,

103
00:03:50,977 --> 00:03:53,660
Michael Schumacher lost a win at the

104
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:55,747
Belgian Grand Prix this way.

105
00:03:56,098 --> 00:03:57,665
It still happens.

106
00:03:57,845 --> 00:04:00,570
At the United States Grand Prix of

107
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:04,978
twenty twenty-three, Lewis Hamilton and Charles Leclerc

108
00:04:04,978 --> 00:04:08,584
were both disqualified after the race because

109
00:04:08,584 --> 00:04:11,069
their planks had worn too much.

110
00:04:11,249 --> 00:04:13,072
Running lower is faster,

111
00:04:13,072 --> 00:04:15,351
but the plank is always there,

112
00:04:15,351 --> 00:04:17,402
grinding away as the proof.

113
00:04:17,582 --> 00:04:19,723
A piece of wood under a machine

114
00:04:19,723 --> 00:04:22,899
worth millions decides who keeps their result.

115
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:25,172
Why does the back of an F1

116
00:04:25,172 --> 00:04:28,056
car open up into these curved channels?

117
00:04:28,236 --> 00:04:29,711
This is the diffuser,

118
00:04:29,711 --> 00:04:32,029
the exit of the tunnels under the

119
00:04:32,029 --> 00:04:32,451
floor.

120
00:04:32,631 --> 00:04:35,259
It gets taller and wider towards the

121
00:04:35,259 --> 00:04:37,523
back, so the fast air under the

122
00:04:37,523 --> 00:04:40,297
car can slow down gradually and rejoin

123
00:04:40,297 --> 00:04:41,977
the air behind the car.

124
00:04:42,157 --> 00:04:45,124
That gentle expansion keeps the air under

125
00:04:45,124 --> 00:04:46,571
the car moving fast,

126
00:04:46,571 --> 00:04:49,321
and keeps the pressure under the floor

127
00:04:49,321 --> 00:04:49,610
low.

128
00:04:49,961 --> 00:04:52,552
The diffuser is so powerful that it

129
00:04:52,552 --> 00:04:55,069
once decided a world championship.

130
00:04:55,249 --> 00:04:56,975
In two thousand nine,

131
00:04:56,975 --> 00:04:59,440
Brawn GP, a team that had been

132
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:02,891
rescued from closure only weeks before the

133
00:05:02,891 --> 00:05:06,095
season, arrived with a double diffuser,

134
00:05:06,095 --> 00:05:08,725
a clever second deck that used a

135
00:05:08,725 --> 00:05:10,121
gap in the rules.

136
00:05:10,301 --> 00:05:12,031
Other teams protested.

137
00:05:12,211 --> 00:05:14,632
The design was declared legal,

138
00:05:14,632 --> 00:05:17,215
and Jenson Button won six of the

139
00:05:17,215 --> 00:05:19,717
first seven races on his way to

140
00:05:19,717 --> 00:05:20,524
the title.

141
00:05:20,874 --> 00:05:24,207
Rival teams copied the idea within months,

142
00:05:24,207 --> 00:05:27,460
and the rules banned double diffusers for

143
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:28,571
twenty eleven.

144
00:05:28,751 --> 00:05:31,592
Since then, the diffuser has been one

145
00:05:31,592 --> 00:05:34,511
of the most tightly regulated areas on

146
00:05:34,511 --> 00:05:35,125
the car.

147
00:05:35,305 --> 00:05:38,128
Every surface is in a box defined

148
00:05:38,128 --> 00:05:39,240
by the rules,

149
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:43,345
and engineers fight over millimetres inside that

150
00:05:43,345 --> 00:05:43,687
box.

151
00:05:43,867 --> 00:05:45,946
It is where the floor's work is

152
00:05:45,946 --> 00:05:46,549
finished.

153
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:48,940
Why is the front wing of an

154
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:51,207
F1 car made of so many layers?

155
00:05:51,387 --> 00:05:53,511
Because it is the first thing that

156
00:05:53,511 --> 00:05:54,510
touches the air.

157
00:05:54,690 --> 00:05:57,072
The front wing makes downforce for the

158
00:05:57,072 --> 00:05:59,265
front wheels, but its bigger job is

159
00:05:59,265 --> 00:06:01,083
to steer the air for the rest

160
00:06:01,083 --> 00:06:01,772
of the car.

161
00:06:01,952 --> 00:06:04,408
It decides how much air goes under

162
00:06:04,408 --> 00:06:06,937
the floor, how the air flows around

163
00:06:06,937 --> 00:06:08,092
the front tyres,

164
00:06:08,092 --> 00:06:10,404
and how it reaches the sidepods.

165
00:06:10,754 --> 00:06:12,600
The tyres are the problem.

166
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:16,042
A spinning, open wheel throws turbulent air

167
00:06:16,042 --> 00:06:19,379
everywhere, and that turbulence can ruin the

168
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:20,593
floor behind it.

169
00:06:20,773 --> 00:06:22,868
So the front wing is shaped to

170
00:06:22,868 --> 00:06:25,173
push that messy air away from the

171
00:06:25,173 --> 00:06:25,452
car.

172
00:06:25,632 --> 00:06:27,420
It is also adjustable.

173
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:28,754
In a pit stop,

174
00:06:28,754 --> 00:06:31,639
mechanics can turn a small screw on

175
00:06:31,639 --> 00:06:34,111
the flaps to change the angle,

176
00:06:34,111 --> 00:06:36,831
and change the balance of the car

177
00:06:36,831 --> 00:06:38,397
for the next stint.

178
00:06:38,747 --> 00:06:41,311
Front wings are also fragile,

179
00:06:41,311 --> 00:06:43,344
and that is on purpose.

180
00:06:43,524 --> 00:06:45,285
They break on contact,

181
00:06:45,285 --> 00:06:48,566
absorbing energy and staying close to the

182
00:06:48,566 --> 00:06:51,367
car instead of turning into a heavy

183
00:06:51,367 --> 00:06:52,487
flying object.

184
00:06:52,667 --> 00:06:55,496
Teams bring many spare wings to each

185
00:06:55,496 --> 00:06:58,326
race, because a broken front wing is

186
00:06:58,326 --> 00:07:00,919
one of the most common damages in

187
00:07:00,919 --> 00:07:01,862
Formula One.

188
00:07:02,042 --> 00:07:04,681
The rules made the wing simpler in

189
00:07:04,681 --> 00:07:07,629
twenty twenty-two, but it is still one

190
00:07:07,629 --> 00:07:10,500
of the most expensive single parts on

191
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:11,121
the car.

192
00:07:11,471 --> 00:07:13,484
Why does the rear wing of an

193
00:07:13,484 --> 00:07:15,569
F1 car open on the straights?

194
00:07:15,749 --> 00:07:17,522
Downforce has a price,

195
00:07:17,522 --> 00:07:18,489
called drag.

196
00:07:18,669 --> 00:07:20,893
The wings that push the car into

197
00:07:20,893 --> 00:07:23,118
the corners also hold it back on

198
00:07:23,118 --> 00:07:24,091
the straights.

199
00:07:24,271 --> 00:07:26,119
From twenty eleven,

200
00:07:26,119 --> 00:07:28,162
Formula One used DRS,

201
00:07:28,162 --> 00:07:30,691
the drag reduction system.

202
00:07:30,871 --> 00:07:33,112
A flap on the rear wing opened

203
00:07:33,112 --> 00:07:35,951
on certain straights when a driver was

204
00:07:35,951 --> 00:07:38,267
within one second of the car in

205
00:07:38,267 --> 00:07:41,181
front, cutting drag and adding speed to

206
00:07:41,181 --> 00:07:42,899
make overtaking easier.

207
00:07:43,249 --> 00:07:45,016
When the flap opens,

208
00:07:45,016 --> 00:07:46,430
the wing stalls.

209
00:07:46,610 --> 00:07:48,620
Less downforce, less drag,

210
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:51,636
and the car gains around ten kilometres

211
00:07:51,636 --> 00:07:53,646
per hour by the end of the

212
00:07:53,646 --> 00:07:54,342
straight.

213
00:07:54,522 --> 00:07:56,621
The moment the driver brakes,

214
00:07:56,621 --> 00:07:59,444
the flap closes again and the downforce

215
00:07:59,444 --> 00:08:01,325
comes back for the corner.

216
00:08:01,505 --> 00:08:03,373
In twenty twenty-six,

217
00:08:03,373 --> 00:08:05,418
new rules went further.

218
00:08:05,598 --> 00:08:08,165
Both the front and the rear wings

219
00:08:08,165 --> 00:08:11,276
now move, opening on straights for every

220
00:08:11,276 --> 00:08:12,754
car to save energy,

221
00:08:12,754 --> 00:08:14,932
and closing for the corners.

222
00:08:15,283 --> 00:08:18,390
Moving wings were banned in Formula One

223
00:08:18,390 --> 00:08:19,346
for decades.

224
00:08:19,526 --> 00:08:21,901
In the late nineteen sixties,

225
00:08:21,901 --> 00:08:24,604
wings on tall stilts broke off at

226
00:08:24,604 --> 00:08:27,388
speed and caused terrible crashes,

227
00:08:27,388 --> 00:08:31,073
and the rule makers banned moving aerodynamic

228
00:08:31,073 --> 00:08:31,728
devices.

229
00:08:31,908 --> 00:08:35,498
DRS returned the idea under strict control,

230
00:08:35,498 --> 00:08:38,921
with a hydraulic actuator that only works

231
00:08:38,921 --> 00:08:41,092
when the system allows it.

232
00:08:41,272 --> 00:08:43,106
The open wing is the tool.

233
00:08:43,286 --> 00:08:45,443
The rule book decides when you may

234
00:08:45,443 --> 00:08:45,887
use it.

235
00:08:46,237 --> 00:08:48,715
Why did old F1 cars have this

236
00:08:48,715 --> 00:08:51,705
forest of small blades on the side?

237
00:08:51,885 --> 00:08:54,001
These were the bargeboards,

238
00:08:54,001 --> 00:08:57,057
a cluster of curved carbon fins between

239
00:08:57,057 --> 00:08:59,722
the front wheels and the sidepods.

240
00:08:59,902 --> 00:09:02,535
Their job was to catch the turbulent

241
00:09:02,535 --> 00:09:05,021
air coming off the front tyres and

242
00:09:05,021 --> 00:09:07,069
send it away from the floor,

243
00:09:07,069 --> 00:09:10,726
while creating small spinning vortices that sealed

244
00:09:10,726 --> 00:09:12,408
the edges of the floor,

245
00:09:12,408 --> 00:09:14,310
like an invisible curtain.

246
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:16,461
By the late twenty tens,

247
00:09:16,461 --> 00:09:19,389
bargeboards had become some of the most

248
00:09:19,389 --> 00:09:21,942
complex parts ever put on a racing

249
00:09:21,942 --> 00:09:24,644
car, with dozens of slots and edges.

250
00:09:24,824 --> 00:09:27,900
They worked brilliantly for the car carrying

251
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:28,249
them.

252
00:09:28,429 --> 00:09:30,557
But they threw the air into a

253
00:09:30,557 --> 00:09:32,098
storm behind the car.

254
00:09:32,278 --> 00:09:35,214
A driver following closely lost a big

255
00:09:35,214 --> 00:09:37,992
part of his downforce and could not

256
00:09:37,992 --> 00:09:39,975
get close enough to pass.

257
00:09:40,326 --> 00:09:43,318
So the rules of twenty twenty-two banned

258
00:09:43,318 --> 00:09:43,693
them.

259
00:09:43,873 --> 00:09:46,542
The new cars were designed around the

260
00:09:46,542 --> 00:09:49,140
floor, with simpler bodywork on top,

261
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:51,665
so that the car behind could follow

262
00:09:51,665 --> 00:09:52,603
more closely.

263
00:09:52,783 --> 00:09:55,319
It is a lesson that keeps returning

264
00:09:55,319 --> 00:09:56,406
in Formula One.

265
00:09:56,586 --> 00:09:58,432
A part can be too good.

266
00:09:58,612 --> 00:10:00,560
When it makes racing worse,

267
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:02,942
the rule makers simply delete it.

268
00:10:03,292 --> 00:10:05,472
Why are the sides of an F1

269
00:10:05,472 --> 00:10:08,156
car shaped in such strange ways?

270
00:10:08,336 --> 00:10:10,245
These are the sidepods,

271
00:10:10,245 --> 00:10:13,398
and inside them are the radiators that

272
00:10:13,398 --> 00:10:14,725
cool the engine,

273
00:10:14,725 --> 00:10:17,380
the battery and the electronics.

274
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:20,255
A Formula One power unit turns a

275
00:10:20,255 --> 00:10:22,108
lot of fuel into heat,

276
00:10:22,108 --> 00:10:24,886
and that heat must leave the car.

277
00:10:25,066 --> 00:10:27,279
The sidepods take in air at the

278
00:10:27,279 --> 00:10:29,920
front, pass it through the radiators,

279
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:31,847
and release it at the back.

280
00:10:32,197 --> 00:10:35,749
But the outside shape is pure aerodynamics.

281
00:10:35,929 --> 00:10:38,485
When the new rules arrived in twenty

282
00:10:38,485 --> 00:10:41,537
twenty-two, each team found its own answer.

283
00:10:41,717 --> 00:10:45,612
Mercedes tried almost no sidepods at all,

284
00:10:45,612 --> 00:10:48,462
a design fans called zero pod.

285
00:10:48,642 --> 00:10:50,186
Ferrari had a wide,

286
00:10:50,186 --> 00:10:51,324
scooped shape.

287
00:10:51,504 --> 00:10:54,446
Red Bull carved a deep undercut below

288
00:10:54,446 --> 00:10:57,069
the sidepod, to send clean air to

289
00:10:57,069 --> 00:10:58,819
the back of the floor.

290
00:10:58,999 --> 00:11:01,035
Red Bull won the most races,

291
00:11:01,035 --> 00:11:03,944
and soon most teams copied the undercut.

292
00:11:04,295 --> 00:11:07,011
Every sidepod is a compromise.

293
00:11:07,191 --> 00:11:10,291
Bigger inlets cool better but add drag.

294
00:11:10,471 --> 00:11:13,908
Smaller ones are faster but the engine

295
00:11:13,908 --> 00:11:17,706
can overheat, especially in hot races like

296
00:11:17,706 --> 00:11:18,610
Singapore.

297
00:11:18,790 --> 00:11:21,259
That is why you sometimes see cars

298
00:11:21,259 --> 00:11:24,019
with extra cooling gills opened up for

299
00:11:24,019 --> 00:11:25,036
a hot weekend.

300
00:11:25,216 --> 00:11:27,530
The shape you see changes from race

301
00:11:27,530 --> 00:11:29,779
to race, depending on the weather.

302
00:11:30,129 --> 00:11:32,567
Why was this black and gold car

303
00:11:32,567 --> 00:11:33,983
almost unbeatable?

304
00:11:34,163 --> 00:11:36,532
This is the Lotus seventy-nine,

305
00:11:36,532 --> 00:11:39,512
and it brought ground effect to Formula

306
00:11:39,512 --> 00:11:39,817
One.

307
00:11:39,997 --> 00:11:43,260
Colin Chapman's team shaped the sidepods like

308
00:11:43,260 --> 00:11:44,565
upside down wings,

309
00:11:44,565 --> 00:11:47,030
and closed the gap between the car

310
00:11:47,030 --> 00:11:49,786
and the track with sliding skirts that

311
00:11:49,786 --> 00:11:51,526
rubbed along the ground.

312
00:11:51,706 --> 00:11:54,029
The air under the car was sealed

313
00:11:54,029 --> 00:11:56,135
in, and the car was sucked to

314
00:11:56,135 --> 00:11:56,861
the track.

315
00:11:57,211 --> 00:11:59,278
In nineteen seventy-eight,

316
00:11:59,278 --> 00:12:02,935
Mario Andretti won the world championship with

317
00:12:02,935 --> 00:12:05,877
it, and his team mate Ronnie Peterson

318
00:12:05,877 --> 00:12:07,149
finished second.

319
00:12:07,329 --> 00:12:10,498
Rival teams rushed to copy the idea.

320
00:12:10,678 --> 00:12:12,965
But ground effect had a dark side.

321
00:12:13,145 --> 00:12:15,497
If a skirt was damaged or the

322
00:12:15,497 --> 00:12:17,200
car went over a kerb,

323
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:20,525
the seal broke and the downforce vanished

324
00:12:20,525 --> 00:12:21,660
in an instant,

325
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:23,525
often in a fast corner.

326
00:12:23,875 --> 00:12:26,801
Cornering speeds rose so fast that the

327
00:12:26,801 --> 00:12:29,342
drivers' bodies took huge forces,

328
00:12:29,342 --> 00:12:32,269
and the accidents became more violent.

329
00:12:32,449 --> 00:12:36,845
Sliding skirts were banned from nineteen eighty-one,

330
00:12:36,845 --> 00:12:39,466
and from nineteen eighty-three,

331
00:12:39,466 --> 00:12:42,340
Formula One required flat bottoms.

332
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:46,499
Ground effect disappeared for almost forty years,

333
00:12:46,499 --> 00:12:49,666
until engineers learned how to bring it

334
00:12:49,666 --> 00:12:52,345
back safely in twenty twenty-two,

335
00:12:52,345 --> 00:12:55,431
with shaped tunnels instead of skirts.

336
00:12:55,781 --> 00:12:58,387
Why does this Formula One car have

337
00:12:58,387 --> 00:13:00,227
a giant fan on its back?

338
00:13:00,407 --> 00:13:03,331
This is the Brabham BT forty-six B,

339
00:13:03,331 --> 00:13:07,675
designed by Gordon Murray in nineteen seventy-eight.

340
00:13:07,855 --> 00:13:10,665
Officially, the fan was there to cool

341
00:13:10,665 --> 00:13:11,501
the engine.

342
00:13:11,681 --> 00:13:14,363
In reality, it sucked the air out

343
00:13:14,363 --> 00:13:15,907
from under the car,

344
00:13:15,907 --> 00:13:18,914
creating a vacuum that pulled the car

345
00:13:18,914 --> 00:13:21,433
down, even at low speed in slow

346
00:13:21,433 --> 00:13:24,603
corners, where wings do almost nothing.

347
00:13:24,953 --> 00:13:26,150
It raced once,

348
00:13:26,150 --> 00:13:29,316
at the Swedish Grand Prix of nineteen

349
00:13:29,316 --> 00:13:30,514
seventy-eight.

350
00:13:30,694 --> 00:13:33,177
Niki Lauda won by more than half

351
00:13:33,177 --> 00:13:33,875
a minute.

352
00:13:34,055 --> 00:13:37,242
Rival drivers complained that the fan threw

353
00:13:37,242 --> 00:13:39,837
dirt and stones at the cars behind.

354
00:13:40,017 --> 00:13:42,613
The car had not broken any rule,

355
00:13:42,613 --> 00:13:45,777
because the fan's main job was declared

356
00:13:45,777 --> 00:13:46,913
to be cooling.

357
00:13:47,093 --> 00:13:49,833
But under pressure from the other teams,

358
00:13:49,833 --> 00:13:52,573
Brabham withdrew it after that one race.

359
00:13:52,923 --> 00:13:55,564
The fan car is still remembered as

360
00:13:55,564 --> 00:13:58,362
one of the greatest tricks in racing

361
00:13:58,362 --> 00:13:58,983
history.

362
00:13:59,163 --> 00:14:01,831
It won every race it ever entered,

363
00:14:01,831 --> 00:14:04,028
because it entered only one.

364
00:14:04,208 --> 00:14:06,216
And the idea never died.

365
00:14:06,396 --> 00:14:09,268
Some modern road and race cars use

366
00:14:09,268 --> 00:14:11,887
fans to pull air from under the

367
00:14:11,887 --> 00:14:12,224
car.

368
00:14:12,404 --> 00:14:13,569
In Formula One,

369
00:14:13,569 --> 00:14:15,977
the rules now say that a part's

370
00:14:15,977 --> 00:14:19,161
main purpose decides whether it is legal.

371
00:14:19,512 --> 00:14:22,617
Why do F1 teams test a smaller

372
00:14:22,617 --> 00:14:24,481
copy of their car?

373
00:14:24,661 --> 00:14:28,063
Because full size wind tunnel testing is

374
00:14:28,063 --> 00:14:29,084
not allowed.

375
00:14:29,264 --> 00:14:31,889
The rules limit teams to models of

376
00:14:31,889 --> 00:14:34,436
at most sixty percent of the real

377
00:14:34,436 --> 00:14:37,369
car, tested in wind tunnels at limited

378
00:14:37,369 --> 00:14:37,832
speed.

379
00:14:38,012 --> 00:14:39,936
Every floor, every wing,

380
00:14:39,936 --> 00:14:42,903
every sidepod you saw today was first

381
00:14:42,903 --> 00:14:45,388
shaped in computer simulations,

382
00:14:45,388 --> 00:14:48,194
then built as a precision model and

383
00:14:48,194 --> 00:14:49,878
tested in the tunnel.

384
00:14:50,228 --> 00:14:52,480
And the tunnel time is rationed.

385
00:14:52,660 --> 00:14:54,786
Since twenty twenty-one,

386
00:14:54,786 --> 00:14:57,710
Formula One uses a sliding scale.

387
00:14:57,890 --> 00:15:00,196
The team that finished last in the

388
00:15:00,196 --> 00:15:03,113
championship gets the most wind tunnel time

389
00:15:03,113 --> 00:15:04,741
and computer simulation,

390
00:15:04,741 --> 00:15:06,911
and the champion gets the least.

391
00:15:07,091 --> 00:15:09,033
It is a handicap system,

392
00:15:09,033 --> 00:15:12,108
designed to let the slower teams catch

393
00:15:12,108 --> 00:15:12,351
up.

394
00:15:12,531 --> 00:15:15,784
Every hour in the tunnel is precious,

395
00:15:15,784 --> 00:15:17,894
so teams plan every run.

396
00:15:18,245 --> 00:15:19,426
On top of that,

397
00:15:19,426 --> 00:15:21,159
there is a budget cap.

398
00:15:21,339 --> 00:15:23,055
Since twenty twenty-one,

399
00:15:23,055 --> 00:15:25,200
teams have a limit on how much

400
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:27,775
they can spend each year on building

401
00:15:27,775 --> 00:15:29,419
and developing the car.

402
00:15:29,599 --> 00:15:31,733
So a new floor is not just

403
00:15:31,733 --> 00:15:33,210
a piece of carbon.

404
00:15:33,390 --> 00:15:35,348
It is wind tunnel hours,

405
00:15:35,348 --> 00:15:38,285
computer time and money taken from a

406
00:15:38,285 --> 00:15:39,427
strict budget.

407
00:15:39,607 --> 00:15:42,282
That is the real reason the floor

408
00:15:42,282 --> 00:15:43,740
is worth millions.

409
00:15:44,091 --> 00:15:45,495
So here are the ten.

410
00:15:45,675 --> 00:15:48,195
A floor that is a hidden wing.

411
00:15:48,375 --> 00:15:51,173
A wooden plank that measures the cheating.

412
00:15:51,353 --> 00:15:54,313
A diffuser that finishes the floor's work.

413
00:15:54,493 --> 00:15:56,688
A front wing that steers the air.

414
00:15:56,868 --> 00:15:59,004
A rear wing that opens.

415
00:15:59,184 --> 00:16:01,301
Blades that were too good,

416
00:16:01,301 --> 00:16:03,662
sidepods that cool and shape,

417
00:16:03,662 --> 00:16:06,267
sliding skirts that were banned,

418
00:16:06,267 --> 00:16:08,628
a fan that won its only race,

419
00:16:08,628 --> 00:16:11,315
and a small model car that shapes

420
00:16:11,315 --> 00:16:12,048
them all.

421
00:16:12,398 --> 00:16:13,617
In Formula One,

422
00:16:13,617 --> 00:16:16,380
the most important part of the car

423
00:16:16,380 --> 00:16:18,087
is the air around it.

424
00:16:18,267 --> 00:16:21,040
Every surface is an argument with that

425
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:23,521
air, and every rule is an argument

426
00:16:23,521 --> 00:16:24,907
with the engineers.

427
00:16:25,087 --> 00:16:28,100
If you want more machines explained part

428
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:30,962
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

429
00:16:31,142 --> 00:16:33,252
And tell us in the comments which

430
00:16:33,252 --> 00:16:35,362
machine you want us to take apart

431
00:16:35,362 --> 00:16:35,682
next.
